t":"Alebouyeh","name":"Alebouyeh, Masoud","rank":42,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"37","first":"Dearbhaile","key":"morrispau","last":"Morris","name":"Morris, Dearbhaile","rank":43,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"37","first":"Louise","key":"oconnorpau","last":"O’Connor","name":"O’Connor, Louise","rank":44,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"37","first":"Martin","key":"cormicanpau","last":"Cormican","name":"Cormican, Martin","rank":45,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"38","first":"Jacob","key":"morangiladpau","last":"Moran-Gilad","name":"Moran-Gilad, Jacob","rank":46,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"39","first":"Antonio","key":"battistipau","last":"Battisti","name":"Battisti, Antonio","rank":47,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"39","first":"Patricia","key":"albapau","last":"Alba","name":"Alba, Patricia","rank":48,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"40","first":"Zeinegul","key":"shakenovapau","last":"Shakenova","name":"Shakenova, Zeinegul","rank":49,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"41","first":"Ciira","key":"kiiyukiapau","last":"Kiiyukia","name":"Kiiyukia, Ciira","rank":50,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32792677"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-020-69869-0","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"13748","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85089383261"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-cf2163af-cacc-4e5c-bdd0-7130aafa2304","ku-a63d7639-3c5c-459d-96e4-d84f76c6182e"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Setting a baseline for global urban virome surveillance in sewage","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-087.30-037264","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Many common illnesses, for reasons that have not been identified, differentially affect men and women. For instance, the autoimmune diseases systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and Sjögren's syndrome affect nine times more women than men1, whereas schizophrenia affects men with greater frequency and severity relative to women2. All three illnesses have their strongest common genetic associations in the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) locus, an association that in SLE and Sjögren's syndrome has long been thought to arise from alleles of the human leukocyte antigen (HLA) genes at that locus3-6. Here we show that variation of the complement component 4 (C4) genes C4A and C4B, which are also at the MHC locus and have been linked to increased risk for schizophrenia7, generates 7-fold variation in risk for SLE and 16-fold variation in risk for Sjögren's syndrome among individuals with common C4 genotypes, with C4A protecting more strongly than C4B in both illnesses. The same alleles that increase risk for schizophrenia greatly reduce risk for SLE and Sjögren's syndrome. In all three illnesses, C4 alleles act more strongly in men than in women: common combinations of C4A and C4B generated 14-fold variation in risk for SLE, 31-fold variation in risk for Sjögren's syndrome, and 1.7-fold variation in schizophrenia risk among men (versus 6-fold, 15-fold and 1.26-fold variation in risk among women, respectively). At a protein level, both C4 and its effector C3 were present at higher levels in cerebrospinal fluid and plasma8,9 in men than in women among adults aged between 20 and 50 years, corresponding to the ages of differential disease vulnerability. Sex differences in complement protein levels may help to explain the more potent effects of C4 alleles in men, women's greater risk of SLE and Sjögren's syndrome and men's greater vulnerability to schizophrenia. These results implicate the complement system as a source of sexual dimorphism in vulnerability to diverse illnesses.","author_count":62,"contributors":["Kamitaki, Nolan","Sekar, Aswin","Handsaker, Robert E.","de Rivera, Heather","Tooley, Katherine","Morris, David L.","Taylor, Kimberly E.","Whelan, Christopher W.","Tombleson, Philip","Loohuis, Loes M.Olde","Ripke, Stephan","Neale, Benjamin M.","Corvin, Aiden","Walters, James T.R.","Farh, Kai How","Holmans, Peter A.","Lee, Phil","Bulik-Sullivan, Brendan","Collier, David A.","Huang, Hailiang","Pers, Tune H.","Agartz, Ingrid","Agerbo, Esben","Albus, Margot","Alexander, Madeline","Amin, Farooq","Bacanu, Silviu A.","Begemann, Martin","Belliveau, Richard A.","Bene, Judit","Bergen, Sarah E.","Bevilacqua, Elizabeth","Bigdeli, Tim B.","Black, Donald W.","Bruggeman, Richard","Buccola, Nancy G.","Buckner, Randy L.","Byerley, William","Cahn, Wiepke","Cai, Guiqing","Cairns, Murray J.","Campion, Dominique","Hansen, Mark","Hansen, Thomas","Li, Tao","Olsen, Line","Pantelis, Christos","Rasmussen, Henrik B.","Werge, Thomas","Su, Zhan"],"cuid":["S02-089.16-018480"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corpau":["Psychosis Endophenotypes International Consortium","Schizophrenia Working Group of the Psychiatric Genomics Consortium","Wellcome Trust Case–Control Consortium 2"],"country":["Australia","France","Germany","Hungary","Ireland","Netherlands","Norway","Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Atlanta Veterans Affairs Medical Center","Cardiff University","Cincinnati Children's Hospital Medical Center","Diakonhjemmet Hospital","Eli Lilly USA","Emory University","Harvard University","Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai","Institut National de la Santé et de la Recherche Médicale (INSERM)","Isar-Amper-Klinikum München-Ost","Karolinska Institutet","King's College London","Louisiana State University Health Sciences Center"