au"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Noël P","key":"burttnoëlppau","last":"Burtt","name":"Burtt, Noël P","rank":298,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 128 1 90","corresponding":null,"first":"Jose C","key":"florezjosecpau","last":"Florez","name":"Florez, Jose C","rank":299,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"boehnkemichaelpau","last":"Boehnke","name":"Boehnke, Michael","rank":300,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"11 5 96","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark I","key":"mccarthymarkipau","last":"McCarthy","name":"McCarthy, Mark I","rank":301,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29257133"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/sdata.2017.179","issn":["20524463"],"journal":"Scientific Data","pages":"170179","paperid":"170179","publisher":"Nature Research","status":"p","title":"Scientific Data","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85038860473"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-159f80be-c417-4ce8-9edb-21038b4de20f","au-e81ae5de-f67e-4d81-8f2a-9e4a5c94926a","ku-4ad7f9a5-5354-436e-a03e-ddfbba97c05a","sdu-b2bf8f61-20b9-41de-b6e7-bfe3a13e6039"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Sequence data and association statistics from 12,940 type 2 diabetes cases and controls","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-099.24-001109","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Background An update of evidence-based guidelines concerning liberation from mechanical ventilation is needed as new evidence has become available. The American College of Chest Physicians (CHEST) and the American Thoracic Society (ATS) have collaborated to provide recommendations to clinicians concerning liberation from the ventilator. Methods Comprehensive evidence syntheses, including meta-analyses, were performed to summarize all available evidence relevant to the guideline panel's questions. The evidence was appraised using the Grading of Recommendations, Assessment, Development, and Evaluation (GRADE) approach, and the results were summarized in evidence profiles. The evidence syntheses were discussed and recommendations developed and approved by a multidisciplinary committee of experts in mechanical ventilation. Results Recommendations for three population, intervention, comparator, outcome (PICO) questions concerning ventilator liberation are presented in this document. The guideline panel considered the balance of desirable (benefits) and undesirable (burdens, adverse effects, costs) consequences, quality of evidence, feasibility, and acceptability of various interventions with respect to the selected questions. Conditional (weak) recommendations were made to use inspiratory pressure augmentation in the initial spontaneous breathing trial (SBT) and to use protocols to minimize sedation for patients ventilated for more than 24 h. A strong recommendation was made to use preventive noninvasive ventilation (NIV) for high-risk patients ventilated for more than 24 h immediately after extubation to improve selected outcomes. The recommendations were limited by the quality of the available evidence. Conclusions The guideline panel provided recommendations for inspiratory pressure augmentation during an initial SBT, protocols minimizing sedation, and preventative NIV, in relation to ventilator liberation.","author_count":22,"contributors":["Ouellette, D